  "Number": 22,
  "Question": "If \\(f(x)\\) is a real-valued function of the real variable \\(x,\\) and \\(f(x)\\) is not identically zero, and for all \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) \\[ \\begin{gathered} f(a+b)+f(a-b)\\\\ =2f(a)+2f(b), \\end{gathered} \\] then for all \\(x\\) and \\(y\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(f(0)=1\\)",
    "B": "\\(f(-x)=-f(x)\\)",
    "C": "\\(f(-x)=f(x)\\)",
    "D": "\\(f(x+y)=f(x)+f(y)\\)",
    "E": "there is a positive number \\(T\\) such that \\(f(x+T)=f(x)\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Solution": "Because the identity holds for every pair of real inputs, we can use zero to simplify its arguments. First set \\(a=b=0\\). Then\n\\[\n2f(0)=4f(0),\n\\]\nso \\(f(0)=0\\).\n\nNow set \\(a=0\\) and \\(b=x\\), where \\(x\\) is any real number. The given identity becomes\n\\[\nf(x)+f(-x)=2f(0)+2f(x)=2f(x).\n\\]\nSubtracting \\(f(x)\\) gives \\(f(-x)=f(x)\\). Thus \\(f\\) is even, and the answer is \\(\\boxed{\\text{C}}\\).",
